# Bytes, not text.
#
# A detached signature covers the exact bytes of a file. Git's end-of-line
# translation is on by default on Windows (`core.autocrlf=true`, which is what
# GitHub's windows-latest runners check out with), so without the rules below a
# checkout there rewrites every LF into CRLF and the file is no longer the file
# that was signed. Nothing looks wrong: the JSON still parses, the text still
# reads, and the signature simply stops verifying with a message
# ("its detached signature does not match its bytes") that reads like sabotage.
#
# Measured, not assumed: `git clone -c core.autocrlf=true` turned the 114-byte
# key-identity fixture into 115 bytes and openssl went from "Verified OK" to
# "Verification Failure". The Windows CI job found it first.
#
# -text, not binary: these files stay diffable and greppable, they just stop
# being translated.

# The pair a channel publishes. The manifest is signed; the .sig is the signature.
channels/*.json -text
channels/*.sig  -text

# The fixture that proves the embedded key is the release key, and its signature.
tests/fixtures/key-identity.txt -text
tests/fixtures/key-identity.sig -text

# The installer and its tests are shell scripts. `curl | sh` is unaffected either
# way -- raw.githubusercontent.com serves the blob as stored -- but a checkout on
# Windows produces `set -eu\r`, and what `sh` says about that is
# "set: Illegal option -", which names nothing an author would recognise.
# Measured in the same autocrlf clone as above.
*.sh -text
